{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D6MUdJQZo3QqdTBbHgUyLn","type":"tool_result","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval targets.\n\nCoverage of Bradley–Terry theory (extensions, existence/consistency, uncertainty) is dense. The existing bibliography does not cover three areas the draft relies on or motivates itself with. The draft concedes this in its \"Scope of the bibliography\" paragraph and in the Extended limitations. Numeric claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are not targets.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Chatbot Arena / MT-Bench pairwise LLM evaluation and the datasets used\n- Influence functions and leave-one-out / Cook's-distance-style deletion diagnostics\n- Manipulation and rigging of crowd-vote or pairwise-comparison rankings\n\n## Targets\n\n1. **arena-leaderboard-context** (Introduction, high, benchmark_context)\n   - Anchor: \"Leaderboards that aggregate 'which of A and B is better' judgments into Bradley–Terry (BT) ratings are used to choose models…\"\n   - Reason: The motivating claim about leaderboard use has no citation. The Data section uses Arena 55k/140k and MT-Bench human judgments without citing the platform or dataset papers.\n   - Search topics: Chatbot Arena platform paper; MT-Bench human judgments / LLM-as-a-judge; Arena human-preference dataset.\n\n2. **influence-deletion-method** (Problem Setup and Method, high, background)\n   - Anchor: \"Deleting row $i$ perturbs the stationarity condition by $-\\nabla\\ell_i$, so a Newton step predicts the change of the gap…\"\n   - Reason: The first-order influence formula with leverage correction is the core of the studied configuration and the basis of the B2 comparator. It has no citation to influence-function or case-deletion literature. Only Broderick et al. is cited.\n   - Search topics: influence functions for black-box predictions; Cook's distance / case deletion in logistic regression; one-step leave-one-out approximations for M-estimators.\n\n3. **manipulation-positioning** (Related Work, medium, positioning)\n   - Anchor: \"Work on adversarial manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards, on evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on influence functions…\"\n   - Reason: The draft motivates the removal budget through deliberate action and rigging, then disclaims any positioning against that work. One or two citations would replace the disclaimer.\n   - Search topics: adversarial vote manipulation of LLM arena leaderboards; robustness of pairwise-comparison rankings to corrupted comparisons.\n\n## Not targeted\nRelated-work paragraphs on BT extensions, estimation and uncertainty are well supported. Adding more there would only grow the bibliography.\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. Six queries, all driven by `draft_reference_gaps.md/json`.\n\n**Central topic:** Citation gaps in a paper on minimal removal sets (verified by exact Bradley-Terry refit) that flip adjacent-pair rankings of pairwise-comparison leaderboards.\n\n## Existing bibliography (context)\nThe bibliography already covers Bradley-Terry extensions, existence and consistency of the MLE, estimation and uncertainty (Davidson, Simons, Butler, Bong, Gao, Li, Yan and others), plus Broderick et al. on dropping data. No query below revisits those areas.\n\n## Queries\n\n### 1. arena-leaderboard-context (Introduction, high)\n- `Chatbot Arena open platform for evaluating LLMs by human preference`, category `benchmark_context`.\n- The opening claim that BT leaderboards are used to choose and announce models, and the Arena 55k/140k data in the Data section, have no platform citation.\n\n### 2. arena-leaderboard-context (Data, high)\n- `Judging LLM-as-a-judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena human pairwise judgments`, category `benchmark_context`.\n- The MT-Bench human judgments and the Arena human-preference data are used without citing the dataset paper.\n\n### 3. influence-deletion-method (Problem Setup and Method, high)\n- `Understanding black-box predictions via influence functions`, category `core_method`.\n- The Newton-step influence of deleting row i is the core of the studied configuration. Only Broderick et al. is cited.\n\n### 4. influence-deletion-method (Problem Setup and Method, high)\n- `Cook's distance case deletion diagnostics one-step approximation logistic regression`, category `baseline_comparator`.\n- The leverage-corrected leave-one-out approximation and the B2 influence-only comparator follow classical deletion diagnostics.\n\n### 5. manipulation-positioning (Related Work, medium)\n- `adversarial vote manipulation of LLM arena leaderboard rankings`, category `result_driven_theme`.\n- The budget is motivated through deliberate action and rigging, yet the \"Scope of the bibliography\" paragraph disclaims positioning against that work.\n\n### 6. manipulation-positioning (Related Work, medium)\n- `robustness of rankings from pairwise comparisons to adversarial or corrupted comparisons`, category `baseline_comparator`.\n- One or two general comparator citations would replace the disclaimer.\n\n## Not targeted\nBT extensions, estimation and uncertainty related work. The gap memo says more citations there would only grow the bibliography. Numeric claims are grounded in `paper_data.json`. No Elo or sports queries are added because the gap artifact does not flag them.\n# ICLR 2026 Venue Brief\n\nUse this brief to tailor the manuscript for the International Conference on Learning\nRepresentations 2026 submission.\n\n## Hard Constraints\n- Target venue: ICLR 2026.\n- Submission type: conference paper.\n- Body-page budget: 8 to 9 pages, excluding references. A body of 8 pages counts only when its final page is at least 75% full; a shorter or sparser body fails validation.\n- Review model: double-blind. The submission must be fully anonymized.\n- Omit author names, affiliations, acknowledgements, and funding statements from the manuscript.\n- Do not include self-identifying links such as personal repositories, lab pages, or internal URLs.\n- Refer to your own prior work in the third person; never write phrasing that reveals authorship.\n- Include a separate `LLM Usage` section, which may appear in the appendix, describing the precise role\n  of LLMs in research ideation and writing. Significant LLM use must be disclosed; nondisclosure can\n  cause desk rejection.\n- The paper must satisfy the ICLR template and formatting rules selected by `--paper-format iclr`.\n\n## Audience\n- Machine learning researchers working on representation learning, optimization, architectures, and\n  empirical methodology.\n- Readers expect a clearly stated contribution, honest baselines, controlled comparisons, ablations\n  that isolate the mechanism, and explicit statements of scope and limitations.\n- Reviewers reward reproducibility and calibrated claims far more than headline numbers.\n\n## Framing Priorities\n- Lead with the research question and the specific claim the evidence supports.\n- State the contribution precisely in the abstract and introduction: what is new, what it improves,\n  and under which conditions.\n- Motivate the method mechanistically. Explain why it should work, not only that it does.\n- Support the central claim with controlled comparisons against meaningful baselines.\n- Use ablations to attribute the gain to the proposed component rather than to incidental changes.\n- Report variance across seeds or runs whenever the artifacts provide it.\n- Be explicit about the regime studied and where the result is not expected to hold.\n\n## Manuscript Guidance\n- Prefer a title and abstract that name the mechanism and the setting, not a generic improvement claim.\n- In the introduction, make the contribution list concrete and verifiable against the artifacts.\n- In the method section, give enough detail to reimplement: objective, architecture, and the\n  hyperparameters that matter.\n- In the results section, foreground controlled comparisons, ablations, sensitivity, and failure modes.\n- Include a limitations discussion that a reviewer would otherwise raise.\n- Figures must meet top-tier conference standards in both design (clean typography, consistent colors,\n  proper labels and legends, high resolution) and scientific content (clear message, no filler).\n- The template is single-column: size figures for the full text width and keep axis labels legible.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n- Do not deanonymize the submission in any way.\n- Do not overclaim generality beyond the settings actually evaluated.\n- Do not present a benchmark delta as a mechanistic insight without evidence connecting the two.\n- Do not omit baselines or ablations that the grounded artifacts already support.\n- Do not describe results as state of the art unless the comparison in the artifacts establishes it.\n- Do not pad the paper to fill the page budget.\n\n## Decision Rule\n- When venue framing and grounded evidence conflict, follow the grounded evidence.\n- Use this brief to choose emphasis, terminology, and narrative framing, not to invent stronger claims\n  than the run supports.\n@Article{Davidson1970OnET,\n title = {On Extending the Bradley-Terry Model to Accommodate Ties in Paired Comparison Experiments},\n@Article{Simons1999AsymptoticsWT,\n title = {Asymptotics when the number of parameters tends to infinity in the Bradley-Terry model for paired comparisons},\n@Article{Davidson1977OnET,\n title = {On Extending the Bradley-Terry Model to Incorporate Within-Pair Order Effects},\n@Article{Gao2021UncertaintyQI,\n title = {Uncertainty quantification in the Bradley–Terry–Luce model},\n@Article{Leonard1977AnAB,\n title = {An Alternative Bayesian Approach to the Bradley-Terry Model for Paired Comparisons},\n@Article{Davidson1969MultivariatePC,\n title = {Multivariate paired comparisons: The extension of a univariate model and associated estimation and test procedures},\n@Article{Yan2011GROUPEDSP,\n title = {GROUPED SPARSE PAIRED COMPARISONS IN THE BRADLEY-TERRY MODEL},\n@Article{Causeur2005A2E,\n title = {A 2-dimensional extension of the Bradley–Terry model for paired comparisons},\n@Article{Bong2021GeneralizedRF,\n title = {Generalized Results for the Existence and Consistency of the MLE in the Bradley-Terry-Luce Model},\n@Article{Stern2011ModeratedPC,\n title = {Moderated paired comparisons: a generalized Bradley–Terry model for continuous data using a discontinuous penalized likelihood function},\n@Article{Fageot2023GeneralizedBM,\n title = {Generalized Bradley-Terry Models for Score Estimation from Paired Comparisons},\n@Article{Genest1999DerivingPF,\n title = {Deriving priorities from the Bradley-Terry model},\n@Article{Butler2004TheEO,\n title = {The existence of maximum likelihood estimates in the Bradley-Terry model and its extensions},\n@Article{Makur2024MinimaxHT,\n title = {Minimax Hypothesis Testing for the Bradley–Terry–Luce Model},\n@Article{Morel-Balbi2025EstimationOP,\n title = {Estimation of partial rankings from sparse, noisy comparisons},\n@Article{Defresne2025PreferenceEF,\n title = {Preference Elicitation for Multi-objective Combinatorial Optimization with Active Learning and Maximum Likelihood Estimation},\n@Inproceedings{Yan2025InferenceIA,\n title = {Inference in a generalized Bradley-Terry model for paired comparisons with covariates and a growing number of subjects},\n@Inproceedings{Li2021ThePO,\n title = {The Performance of the MLE in the Bradley-Terry-Luce Model in (cid:96) ∞ -Loss and under General Graph Topologies},\n@Inproceedings{Selby2024PageRankAT,\n title = {PageRank and the Bradley-Terry model},\n@Article{Varin2024TractableRR,\n title = {Tractable Ridge Regression for Paired Comparisons},\n@Article{Vojnović2019ConvergenceRO,\n title = {Convergence Rates of Gradient Descent and MM Algorithms for Generalized Bradley-Terry Models},\n@Inproceedings{Hussain2013BayesianIO,\n title = {Bayesian Inference of Log-linear Version of the Bradley-Terry Model for Paired Comparisons Using Uninformative Prior},\n@Inproceedings{Sawadogo2019CombiningMA,\n title = {Combining MM-Algorithms and MCMC Procedures for Maximum Likelihood Estimation in Mallows-Bradley-Terry Models},\n@Inproceedings{Rizvi2026BayesianBF,\n title = {Bayesian Bradley-Terry Framework for Ranking Multi-Indicator Entities: Application to Indian States and Union Territories},\n@Inproceedings{Broderick2020AnAF,\n title = {An Automatic Finite-Sample Robustness Metric: When Can Dropping a Little Data Make a Big Difference?},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132,\n  title = {Chatbot Arena: An Open Platform for Evaluating LLMs by Human Preference},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2502.09328,\n  title = {Copilot Arena: A Platform for Code LLM Evaluation in the Wild},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2407.10627,\n  title = {Arena Learning: Build Data Flywheel for LLMs Post-training via Simulated Chatbot Arena},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2509.11026,\n  title = {Rethinking Human Preference Evaluation of LLM Rationales},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2406.12624,\n  title = {Judging the Judges: Evaluating Alignment and Vulnerabilities in LLMs-as-Judges},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2406.07791,\n  title = {Judging the Judges: A Systematic Study of Position Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2412.05579,\n  title = {LLMs-as-Judges: A Comprehensive Survey on LLM-based Evaluation Methods},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2410.21819,\n  title = {Self-Preference Bias in LLM-as-a-Judge},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2402.04788,\n  title = {MLLM-as-a-Judge: Assessing Multimodal LLM-as-a-Judge with Vision-Language Benchmark},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730,\n  title = {Understanding Black-box Predictions via Influence Functions},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2312.05586,\n  title = {Generalized Influence Functions for Better Model Change Estimates},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1911.00418,\n  title = {On Second-Order Group Influence Functions for Black-Box Predictions},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1810.10118,\n  title = {Interpreting Black Box Predictions using Fisher Kernels},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1602.02658,\n  title = {Graying the black box: Understanding DQNs},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.00810,\n  title = {Opening the Black Box of Deep Neural Networks via Information},\n@phdthesis{https://doi.org/10.17615/9sde-5372,\n  title = {Bayesian Influence Diagnostic Methods for Parametric Regression Models},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858,\n  title = {Improving Your Model Ranking on Chatbot Arena by Vote Rigging},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2605.06656,\n  title = {Why Global LLM Leaderboards Are Misleading: Small Portfolios for Heterogeneous Supervised ML},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2510.06525,\n  title = {Text-to-Image Models Leave Identifiable Signatures: Implications for Leaderboard Security},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2601.09647,\n  title = {Identifying Models Behind Text-to-Image Leaderboards},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2503.08704,\n  title = {Life-Cycle Routing Vulnerabilities of LLM Router},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2504.18838,\n  title = {Toward Generalizable Evaluation in the LLM Era: A Survey Beyond Benchmarks},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2603.29403,\n  title = {Security in LLM-as-a-Judge: A Comprehensive SoK},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2404.01015,\n  title = {PairEval: Open-domain Dialogue Evaluation with Pairwise Comparison},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2011.01104,\n  title = {Efficient PAC Learning from the Crowd with Pairwise Comparisons},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2502.08077,\n  title = {Cascading Bandits Robust to Adversarial Corruptions},\n@misc{https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2005.13748,\n  title = {Calibrated Surrogate Losses for Adversarially Robust Classification},\n\n- Reference query plan (2026-11-11): wrote 10 queries on Bradley-Terry, AMIP/influence-based data-dropping robustness, Arena/MT-Bench/NBA-Elo data context, vote rigging, leaderboard illusion, rank-aggregation robustness, ranking uncertainty. Chosen over generic benchmarking/optimization queries because they match methods and datasets actually run; no query targets the withheld paper.\n\n- Paper strategy (2026-11-11): chose a thesis-first measurement paper on refit-verified removal budgets for BT leaderboards (crowd vs sports final, expert search-only). Rejected method-superiority (selected == influence-only B2 on all 4 final cases; search delta 0.0, 3/3 scored) and run-diary framings. Final outcome qualified is reported as achieved, not as superiority. Gap noted: references.bib lacks Arena/MT-Bench/influence/rigging entries.\n\n- Paper draft (2026-11-11): wrote paper.tex (ICLR, anonymous, 9 body pages, SI incl. LLM Usage), claims_grounding.json (25 claims), figure_manifest.json (protocol_overview placeholder owned by orchestrator; main_budget_by_case and controls_permutation via scripts/build_figures.py). Rejected framings: method superiority (selected == B2 everywhere), run diary. Added derived normaliser (2N/M) showing crowd-vs-sports direction reverses; top-pair share ratio equals table-size ratio (both 62 rows). Rewrote/removed: any claim on c02/c03 implementation (no source evidence), NBA 'regular season' (unknown), starting-baseline held-out comparison (not run). Citation gap: bib lacks Arena/MT-Bench/influence/rigging works; stated openly in related work and SI. Evidence verifier: 0 dangling; validate_claims passes. Concept-figure placeholder guarded by .concept_figure_complete.\n### 2026-11-11T08:09:23.528498+00:00 | step=paper | scope=paper\n- summary: Final paper written: bounded descriptive measurement of refit-verified removal budgets (crowd 62/4 rows, NBA 62/8 rows); studied config equals