{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgvog3nR42CdxMAAi7ZVE","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01152ktvxNgnsygLXkxpDV6U","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\np='paper.tex'; s=open(p).read()\nA='arxiv.2403.04132'; K='https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.1703.04730'; M='https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2501.17858'; C='https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2403.04132'\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a\n    s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems. The BT\",\n    \"are used to choose models, to announce releases, and to compare research systems; Chatbot Arena is a prominent crowd-vote example \\\\citep{%s}. The BT\"%C)\nrep(\"The perturbation scale and the cross-dataset\", \"The perturbation scale and the cross-dataset\")\nrep(\"a Newton step predicts the change of the gap $g=\\\\theta_{\\\\hi}-\\\\theta_{\\\\lo}$ as\",\n    \"a Newton step predicts the change of the gap $g=\\\\theta_{\\\\hi}-\\\\theta_{\\\\lo}$ as, in the manner of influence-function analyses of the effect of removing a training point \\\\citep{%s},\"%K)\nrep(\"Rows with the most negative $\\\\Delta_i$ close the gap fastest to first order.\",\n    \"Rows with the most negative $\\\\Delta_i$ close the gap fastest to first order. Our use of this approximation differs from a pure influence analysis in that it only orders candidate rows, and every set is then verified by an exact refit.\")\nrep(\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Scope of the bibliography.} Work on adversarial manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards, on evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on influence functions for black-box predictions is relevant background. It is not part of the verified reference set for this submission, so we do not position our measurements against it and make no claim of novelty relative to it.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"\\\\paragraph{Manipulation of crowd-vote leaderboards.} \\\\citet{%s} study vote-rigging strategies against Chatbot Arena and their effect on model rankings. Their setting is adversarial and allows an actor to add votes, whereas ours is a deletion-only perturbation checked by exact refit, with no adversary model, so the two are complementary rather than comparable and we do not compare numbers. Influence-based analysis of single training points is the tool behind our row ordering \\\\citep{%s}. Work on other evaluation practices of arena-style platforms, and on MT-Bench-style model-judge evaluation, is relevant background that is not in our verified reference set, and we make no claim of novelty relative to it.\"\"\"%(M,K))\nrep(\"The held-out crowd table is the Chatbot Arena 140k preference release\",\n    \"The held-out crowd table is the Chatbot Arena 140k preference release \\\\citep{%s}\"%C)\nrep(\"The bibliography available for this submission lacks work on vote manipulation, on arena-platform practices and on influence functions, so positioning relative to those lines is absent.\",\n    \"The bibliography available for this submission includes one work on vote rigging and one on influence functions but lacks the MT-Bench paper, classical case-deletion diagnostics and work on arena-platform practices beyond the platform description, so positioning relative to those lines is incomplete.\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nE\ngrep -n \"cited\\|arena-platform\" paper.tex | head; python3 -c \"\nimport json;d=json.load(open('claims_grounding.json'));print(d['claims'][0]);print(len(d['claims']))\"; ls scripts derived; ls .claude/skills 2>/dev/null"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2078,"cache_read_input_tokens":67926,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2078},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86df7501-87c0-4521-a0c1-fa3de50975c9","uuid":"c4fbc476-9959-4979-9b55-9abdacc2f6ca","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:13:44.427Z","request_id":"req_011Cgvog3DwAGG3fbWpKEbMd"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01152ktvxNgnsygLXkxpDV6U","type":"tool_result","content":"319:Large language models were used throughout this work. The search configurations and their code, the analysis scripts, and the text of this manuscript were produced with an LLM-based research assistant operating inside an automated pipeline that proposed configurations, ran a development evaluation, froze one selection, and then ran a sealed held-out evaluation whose results the manuscript reports. The assistant also drafted the related-work discussion from a small retrieved bibliography. Every figure and table in this paper is derived by script from the evaluator's recorded outputs. No claim in the paper relies on an LLM's unverified recollection of the literature; related work is described only at the level of the cited titles.\n322:Four held-out cases on two designs, two identical episodes and twenty random draws are the entire basis for the held-out claims. The development side adds one expert table of six participants. We did not run exhaustive or exact optimisation on any table, so we cannot say how far the certified budgets are from the minimum. We did not examine whether the reversed pairs are statistically distinguishable under sampling uncertainty, which would be the natural reading of an adjacent-pair flip for a practitioner. The bibliography available for this submission includes one work on vote rigging and one on influence functions but lacks the MT-Bench paper, classical case-deletion diagnostics and work on arena-platform practices beyond the platform description, so positioning relative to those lines is incomplete.\n{'claim': 'Held-out crowd table has 135,630 rows, 52 models, cap 2,713; NBA table has 12,900 rows, 30 teams, cap 258.', 'source_ids': ['final.evidence.episodes']}\n25\nderived:\nfinal_stats.json\nfinal_stats.json.prov.json\ntask_meta\n\nscripts:\nbuild_figures.py\nderive_final_stats.py\ngenerate-concept-figure\nmemory-log\nretrieve-references\nscientific-evidence\nvalidate-paper\nwrite-paper-standalone\n[This command modified 1 file you've previously read: paper.tex. Call Read before editing.]","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"86df7501-87c0-4521-a0c1-fa3de50975c9","uuid":"36e6bd6f-2ee4-41c4-a4de-0878005377ed","timestamp":"2026-11-11T08:13:44.568Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"319:Large language models were used throughout this work. The search configurations and their code, the analysis scripts, and the text of this manuscript were produced with an LLM-based research assistant operating inside an automated pipeline that proposed configurations, ran a development evaluation, froze one selection, and then ran a sealed held-out evaluation whose results the manuscript reports. The assistant also drafted the related-work discussion from a small retrieved bibliography. Every figure and table in this paper is derived by script from the evaluator's recorded outputs. No claim in the paper relies on an LLM's unverified recollection of the literature; related work is described only at the level of the cited titles.\n322:Four held-out cases on two designs, two identical episodes and twenty random draws are the entire basis for the held-out claims. The development side adds one expert table of six participants. We did not run exhaustive or exact optimisation on any table, so we cannot say how far the certified budgets are from the minimum. We did not examine whether the reversed pairs are statistically distinguishable under sampling uncertainty, which would be the natural reading of an adjacent-pair flip for a practitioner. The bibliography available for this submission includes one work on vote rigging and one on influence functions but lacks the MT-Bench paper, classical case-deletion diagnostics and work on arena-platform practices beyond the platform description, so positioning relative to those lines is incomplete.\n{'claim': 'Held-out crowd table